<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="using:System"
                    xmlns:ease="using:Avalonia.Animation.Easings">

  <!--
    The shell's whole vocabulary. Nothing outside this file may write a literal
    colour, size, radius or duration.

    Four rules the values encode, all learned the hard way in dark tool UIs:

    1. STRUCTURAL LINES ARE SOLID HEX, NEVER ALPHA. A white-at-8% hairline
       resolves to a different rendered colour over each surface it crosses, so
       one border reads as two. Alpha is reserved for hover and selection
       FILLS, which are transient and sit on a known background.

    2. TEXT COLOUR IS A COLOUR, NEVER AN OPACITY. Opacity on a white brush
       breaks Windows subpixel rendering and gamma, which shows up as fringed,
       slightly bolder text that nobody can point at.

    3. SURFACES HAVE A DIRECTION, AND THE DIRECTION IS THE AFFORDANCE. Anything
       you PRESS is lighter than the panel it sits on; anything you TYPE INTO is
       darker. Before this rule existed, a mode chip and a text field shared a
       fill, a border, a radius and a font, and differed only by being four
       pixels apart in height - so two buttons sat in the toolbar dressed as
       inputs, beside three real inputs, and nothing on screen said which was
       which. Raised means press, sunken means type, and there is no third
       reading to learn.

    4. EVERY BRUSH IS A SolidColorBrush OVER A DynamicResource COLOUR. The
       consumers keep referencing brushes with StaticResource, so a theme swap
       re-tints every brush IN PLACE and no reference anywhere else in the shell
       has to change or be re-resolved. The expensive route - converting all 380
       consumer references to DynamicResource - buys the same thing and costs a
       live subscription per reference; this costs about thirty lines here and
       nothing anywhere else.
  -->

  <!-- ___ Ground surfaces _________________________________
       Seven steps, each about four L* apart, and the ladder itself is the
       change: the shell used to sit at #0A0708 to #1B1819, which is four
       near-black surfaces separated by 1.06:1 with the hairlines carrying the
       whole layout.

       THE VALUE, NOT THE HUE, IS WHAT MADE IT READ AS A DARK WEB PAGE. Near
       black beside a tone-mapped viewport whose baseplate sits around L* 65 is
       a ~25:1 slam, and the eye adapts to the largest bright field in the
       window, which is the render. Adapted there, every state change in the
       chrome landed in the crushed bottom of the response curve - which is why
       the design looked right in a screenshot (no adapting field) and dead on a
       monitor. Unreal, Unity, Godot and Blender all sit at L* 15 to 33 for
       exactly this reason: the chrome must be quieter than the picture, not a
       different medium from it.

       Warm-neutral rather than the blue-grey most DCC tools use, because the
       brand accent is warm and a warm red on a cool ground is the gamer-RGB
       look. At L* 4 the old palette's documented warmth was below threshold and
       rendered as neutral black anyway: hue needs value to show. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgWindowColor">#131011</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgAppColor">#1A1718</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgPanelColor">#242021</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgRaisedColor">#2E2A2B</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgWindow" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgWindowColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgApp" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgAppColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgPanel" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgPanelColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgRaised" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgRaisedColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Directional surfaces ____________________________
       Rule 3 above, as four brushes. Control sits ABOVE the panel, input BELOW
       it, and the gap between them is now 2.1:1 of honest value rather than a
       ratio a caption had to explain. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgControlColor">#383335</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgControlHoverColor">#433D3F</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgControlActiveColor">#4E4749</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBgInputColor">#161314</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgControl" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgControlColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgControlHover" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgControlHoverColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgControlActive" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgControlActiveColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBgInput" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgInputColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Borders _________________________________________
       Trim now, not scaffolding. They used to be DARKER than the surfaces they
       separated, because at L* 4 there was nowhere below to go, so every edge
       in the window was a gap. Lighter than their surface reads as a highlight
       edge, which is what a raised control's rim actually is. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBorderSoftColor">#322D2F</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBorderStrongColor">#3F393B</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraBorderControlColor">#4C4547</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBorderSoft" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBorderSoftColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBorderStrong" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBorderStrongColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraBorderControl" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBorderControlColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Viewport bezel __________________________________
       The one place allowed near-black, and it is a deliberate two-step frame
       rather than a cliff: chrome, then bezel, then render. Every DCC tool
       frames its viewport, and this is why - an unframed render butted straight
       against a panel makes the panel look like a bug. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraViewportBezel" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraBgWindowColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Text ____________________________________________
       Contrast against the PANEL (#242021), which is where nearly all of it is
       read: emphasis 13.4:1, body 8.3:1, muted 4.8:1, faint 2.7:1.

       Muted passing AA is new. It sat at 4.3:1 and was carrying the inspector's
       section headings; a heading that fails the small-text threshold is a
       disabled row that happens to be a heading.

       Disabled does two jobs: genuinely disabled controls, and the
       filter-dimmed tree rows. The old value was 1.5:1 against the old panel -
       literally invisible - in a feature whose entire premise is "dimmed, not
       hidden". -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraTextEmphasisColor">#EFECED</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraTextBodyColor">#C2BBBD</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraTextMutedColor">#958D8F</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraTextDisabledColor">#6A6365</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraTextEmphasis" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraTextEmphasisColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraTextBody" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraTextBodyColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraTextMuted" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraTextMutedColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraTextDisabled" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraTextDisabledColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Accent, and the one thing it is allowed to mean __
       SELECTION. Selected rows, the 2px row bar, the drop indicator, the focus
       ring, one primary button. Budget: under 5% of on-screen pixels, never a
       panel or a toolbar fill.

       It used to mean seven things at once - selected, live tool, focus,
       primary, error, drop target, no-match - which made the window monochrome
       plus one, and made "you picked this thing" and "the machine is in this
       mode" indistinguishable at a glance. The other meanings moved out below.

       SpectraAccentRest is the fill for anything carrying white text: white on
       #E8474A is 4.0:1 and fails AA at 13px, on #C8353A it is 5.2:1. The
       brighter red is that button's hover, not its resting state. And
       SpectraTextDanger exists because the accent AS TEXT is 4.0:1 on the
       panel, which is not an error message. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraAccentColor">#E8474A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraAccentRestColor">#C8353A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraTextDangerColor">#FF6E6B</Color>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraAccent" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraAccentColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraAccentRest" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraAccentRestColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraTextDanger" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraTextDangerColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Mode ____________________________________________
       STATE, not selection: the live gizmo tool, snap on, the dirty dot, a
       non-default chip, and every warning. 7.0:1 on panel, so it reads as a lit
       glyph rather than a shout.

       Warning is the SAME colour deliberately, not a second amber. A warning
       and a lit mode are one semantic family - attention, not error - and two
       ambers four degrees apart would be a distinction nobody could make. A
       warning is told apart by its icon and its place, not by its hue. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraModeColor">#E5A93F</Color>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraMode" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraModeColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraWarning" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraModeColor}" />

  <!-- ___ Semantic ________________________________________
       The brand accent IS red, so destructive actions must not be filled red
       or "delete" and "selected" look identical. Filled red always means
       primary-and-safe; destructive is a ghost with a red border. -->
  <Color x:Key="SpectraSuccessColor">#5DBE7A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SpectraInfoColor">#6E9FDE</Color>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraSuccess" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraSuccessColor}" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraInfo" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraInfoColor}" />

  <!-- ___ The machine's accent, overruled _________________
       Fluent resolves SystemAccentColor from Windows personalisation, so any
       stock control the shell has not explicitly restyled paints itself in
       whatever colour the user picked for their taskbar. The pipeline
       dropdown's selected row was arriving lavender. Naming the family here
       means the leak, wherever the next one turns up, leaks brand red instead
       of somebody's wallpaper.

       Only the first key is named anywhere in this repo. The six shades below
       are read by Fluent's own control templates and by nothing else, so a
       search for references finds none: they are load-bearing and invisible,
       which is exactly the shape of thing a tidy-up deletes as dead. -->
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColor">#C8353A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark1">#B22F33</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark2">#8E2529</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorDark3">#6B1C1F</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight1">#E8474A</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight2">#EE6C6E</Color>
  <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColorLight3">#F49092</Color>

  <!-- ___ Transient state fills ___________________________
       Alpha is correct here: these are localised, transient, and sit on a
       known surface.

       THE OPACITIES WENT UP WITH THE GROUND, AND HAD TO. A 4.5% white lift is a
       fixed ABSOLUTE delta, and by Weber's law a fixed delta reads as LESS
       change on a lighter ground, not more - so carrying the old numbers onto
       the new surfaces would have made every hover in the window quieter than
       it was before. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraRowHover" Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.07" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraRowSelected" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraAccentColor}" Opacity="0.22" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraControlHover" Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.08" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraControlPressed" Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.13" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraControlChecked" Color="{DynamicResource SpectraModeColor}" Opacity="0.22" />

  <!-- ___ Axis identity ___________________________________
       X, Y and Z wear the same hues here as the gizmo handles do in the
       viewport (GizmoColors: 1,0.25,0.25 / 0.25,1,0.25 / 0.35,0.55,1), so the
       letter beside a field and the arrow under the cursor are recognisably
       the same axis.

       Desaturated on purpose. The viewport values are full-strength display
       colours drawn over tone-mapped 3D; at panel scale they would read as
       three more accents, and #FF4040 in particular is close enough to the
       brand red to be mistaken for a selection. These keep the hue and give
       up the shout - lifted one step with the ground, since a tint tuned
       against near-black goes muddy on a mid-dark panel. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraAxisX" Color="#D9635F" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraAxisY" Color="#74B863" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraAxisZ" Color="#6F9BDB" />

  <!-- ___ Node-kind tints _________________________________
       A tree of two hundred identical rows is a list of names; the tint plus
       the glyph is what makes it a scene. Deliberately desaturated except the
       two that matter most at a glance: a light, and a subtractive brush that
       renders nothing at all and can only be found here.

       These are also the shell's only spent information colour, and until now
       they appeared in exactly one place - a 15px tree glyph. They belong on
       every surface that names a kind. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindGroup" Color="#958D8F" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindMesh" Color="#BDB6B8" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindBrushWorld" Color="#8FB4E2" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindBrushPart" Color="#5FCECE" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindBrushSubtractive" Color="#CE8DE0" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindLight" Color="#E9CA4C" />
  <!-- The third saturated one, for the same reason a cut is saturated: an
       entity draws nothing in the viewport at all, so the tree is the only
       place one can be found. Green is the only hue this palette had not
       spent - blue, cyan, magenta and yellow are taken, amber is the shell's
       state colour and red is selection. It is lighter and less saturated than
       SpectraSuccess (#5DBE7A), which is spent on exactly one thing, the filled
       play glyph in the far corner of the command row. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SpectraKindEntity" Color="#7FC58F" />

  <!-- ___ Fonts ___________________________________________
       $Default is Avalonia's terminal fallback. There is no CSS-style
       "monospace" or "sans-serif" generic: naming one resolves to nothing and
       fails silently, which is what the old Consolas,Menlo,monospace stack was
       doing on this machine.

       Mono renders one px smaller than adjacent sans, because mono faces carry
       a larger x-height for the same nominal size and otherwise read bigger.
       Mono is for VALUES - numbers, paths, key names - and never for prose. A
       sentence set in 11px Cascadia Mono is the single most machine-made
       typographic choice a tool can make, and the status bar was doing it. -->
  <FontFamily x:Key="SpectraFontUi">avares://Avalonia.Fonts.Inter/Assets#Inter, Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI, $Default</FontFamily>
  <FontFamily x:Key="SpectraFontMono">Cascadia Mono, Consolas, $Default</FontFamily>

  <!-- ___ Type scale ______________________________________
       13px is the base, which is where every professional tool lives (VS Code
       13, Unity 12, Blender 11). Never 700 weight at 13px on dark: it blooms.

       SpectraFontMicro survives for the two places a genuinely tertiary label
       is wanted. It is NO LONGER the inspector's section headings: 10px
       letter-spaced uppercase muted is the marketing eyebrow, and tools set
       their section headings at body size and let weight carry the emphasis.
       "Transform" beats "T R A N S F O R M". -->
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontMicro">10</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontSmall">11</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontSecondary">12</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontBase">13</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontTitle">14</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFontDisplay">16</x:Double>

  <!-- ___ Fixed heights ___________________________________
       Values, not padding-derived, so rows stack cleanly at 125% and 150% DPI.

       ONE control height for the whole command bar. The old set had toolbar
       buttons at 28 and the fields between them at 24, which ran four pixels
       of misalignment through the densest row in the window.

       The tree row went 22 to 24: at 13px text with a 15px glyph, 22 is the
       dense-web row height, and a scene tree is scanned rather than read. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraTreeRowHeight">24</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraFieldHeight">26</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraControlHeight">28</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraToolbarButtonSize">28</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraMenuBarHeight">32</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraToolbarHeight">40</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpectraStatusBarHeight">26</x:Double>

  <!-- ___ Radii ___________________________________________
       Two values, and they mean different things rather than being two sizes
       of one thing: controls and chips are nearly square, anything FLOATING
       above the window is properly rounded. Rounded flat pills in a row is the
       web idiom; Hammer, Unreal and Blender all set their headers and buttons
       square. The frame and the docked edges carry no radius at all, which is
       said by not setting one rather than by a token nothing could reference.

       Named for their job, not their pixel count: the values moved once and the
       old SpectraRadiusControl/4 keys immediately described neither. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="SpectraRadiusControl">2</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="SpectraRadiusFloat">6</CornerRadius>

  <!-- ___ Motion __________________________________________
       The shell had none. Not "sparse" - zero Transitions, Animations,
       KeyFrames or Easings anywhere in it, so every state in the application
       was a single-frame swap.

       That matters twice over, and the second reason is the one that gets
       missed: a small value step delivered INSTANTLY is a step function with no
       transient, and peripheral vision is motion-driven. The same step
       delivered over 90ms is an event you notice without looking at it. Raising
       the hover fills without adding the ramp would have fixed half of it.

       THE BUDGET, stated so this cannot drift into bouncy: interaction motion
       is at most 130ms; exactly two non-interaction transitions may exceed it
       (a view swap and a reveal scroll) because neither answers a pointer;
       nothing overshoots, so BackEase, BounceEase and ElasticEase are banned by
       name; and nothing loops or idles.

       EXITS ARE FASTER THAN ENTRANCES. Leaving at 55ms while arriving at 90 is
       what stops a list feeling sticky when the pointer sweeps down it.

       AND NOTHING ON THE DRAG PATH ANIMATES. A transition on a value a pointer
       writes every frame is a lag machine: the target moves before the
       interpolation lands, so the element trails the cursor by exactly the
       duration. The drop indicators, every property value cell, the status
       readouts and the rename box are named exclusions. -->
  <ease:CubicEaseOut x:Key="SpectraEaseOut" />
  <ease:CubicEaseInOut x:Key="SpectraEaseInOut" />

  <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="SpectraMotionExit">00:00:00.0550000</sys:TimeSpan>
  <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="SpectraMotionFast">00:00:00.0900000</sys:TimeSpan>
  <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="SpectraMotionBase">00:00:00.1300000</sys:TimeSpan>
  <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="SpectraMotionSwap">00:00:00.1900000</sys:TimeSpan>

</ResourceDictionary>
